<h1>Meet the Neighbors — Finance &amp; Taxation</h1>

<p class="domain-lead">These profiles are illustrative composites. The numbers — incomes, tax burdens, credit amounts, refund eligibility — are derived from current law applied to documented PA-3 conditions. The neighborhoods are real and their statistical character is real. The people are constructed to make the structural patterns visible at the scale of a household. They have no names and are not based on any identifiable individual. They are devices for seeing what current policy means for a household at a specific address — what the system produces for households like this one.</p>

<aside class="construction-note">
<p><strong>Why this form.</strong> The structural finding is what's verified, not any individual story. A statement attributed to a real named person would be either fabricated or cherry-picked. The composite says: this is the typical experience of a household in this position. The "Composite" tag travels with each card so the framing doesn't fade as you scroll.</p>
</aside>

<div data-mode-root data-mode="neutral" data-design="off" data-gap="off">

